WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.590
- So the question is
can Twitter actually ban

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.650
the President of the United
States from its platform

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.430
and a whole bunch of the
president's supporters?

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.581
Well, do you think that
you could ban someone

00:00:09.581 --> 00:00:11.361
from making a speech inside

00:00:11.361 --> 00:00:14.810
of your home because that's
kind of the same issue.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.410
Twitter is a private company

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.710
and the First Amendment doesn't
prohibit private companies

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.240
or individuals from controlling
their own private property.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.500
Frankly, if the government could control

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.619
what a company like Twitter says,

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:29.660
that probably itself would be a violation

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.510
of the First Amendment.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.980
So what Twitter has done in
banning a whole bunch of people

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.270
doesn't violate the First Amendment

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.710
and it doesn't violate the
Communications Decency Act,

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.543
section 230.

00:00:38.543 --> 00:00:40.720
In fact CDA 230 has nothing to do

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.030
with this whatsoever.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:42.863
But anyway,

00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:44.100
stay tuned for more election coverage

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.723
and I'll see you in court.

